WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.120
If acceptance speeches have taught us anything,&nbsp;
the number one person most actors want to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.839
please is their mom. But what happens if&nbsp;
they're in something a little too risqué&nbsp;or violent? 

00:00:10.839 --> 00:00:16.080
With that in mind, here are the&nbsp;
roles that got actors in trouble with their moms.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.840
Since 2016's Captain America: Civil War, Tom&nbsp;
Holland has been best known as Spider-Man,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:27.360
probably the most earnest and innocent superhero&nbsp;
in the MCU. But as much as he's enjoyed playing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:32.160
the iconic web-slinger, he's also attempted&nbsp;
to branch out into a broader range of roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:36.960
And the 2021 crime drama Cherry&nbsp;
couldn't be more of a contrast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.080
much to his mom's horror. Holland plays the&nbsp;
title character, a veteran of the Iraq War&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.855
who self-medicates his PTSD with heroin and&nbsp;
ends up robbing banks to fund his drug habit.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.855 --> 00:00:52.160
During filming, the actor sent his mother a&nbsp;
video of an especially grueling scene in which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:57.440
his character is going through withdrawal on the&nbsp;
floor of a prison cell. As he recalled to Esquire,

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:00.720
"Biggest mistake ever [...]&nbsp;
She was furious with me."

00:01:00.720 --> 00:01:03.680
Holland realized a little too&nbsp;
late that what his mom likes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:06.351
about his movies are the parts that make her go:

00:01:06.427 --> 00:01:07.668
"That's my little boy."

00:01:07.668 --> 00:01:11.280
Alas, Cherry doesn't have anything&nbsp;
like that. Holland considers the film&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:15.600
the hardest role of his career, and the&nbsp;
same can be said for his mom. Luckily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.600 --> 00:01:20.160
she eventually came around, as his parents were&nbsp;
able to enjoy it and be proud of their son.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.840
Most parents would probably struggle to watch&nbsp;
their kids playing a character who gets brutally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:30.560
tortured and killed on screen. But watching them&nbsp;
carry out the torturing and killing themselves&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.560 --> 00:01:36.240
can also provoke some strong emotions. Kathy&nbsp;
Bates has become a TV legend in recent years&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.280
thanks to her American Horror Story performances.&nbsp;
But before she was torturing enslaved people in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.440
Coven or terrifying the residents of Outpost 3 in&nbsp;
Apocalypse, she played obsessed fan Annie Wilkes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:53.200
in the Stephen King adaptation Misery. In the&nbsp;
movie, Annie traps Paul Sheldon, her favorite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.840
author, in her house and forces him to write&nbsp;
a new book the way that she wants it to go.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:01.920
Even if you've never seen the movie or read&nbsp;
the novel it's based on, you probably know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.880
about the most notorious scene in which Annie&nbsp;
breaks Paul's ankles with a sledgehammer.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:10.400
Annie is widely considered one of the&nbsp;
greatest movie villains of all time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:14.053
and Bates even won an Oscar for her performance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.053 --> 00:02:19.600
But her mother wasn't exactly on board. As&nbsp;
she revealed on an episode of Conan in 2017,

00:02:19.600 --> 00:02:22.800
"She saw it, and she said she didn't&nbsp;
know what all the fuss was about,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.901
that she had actually seen that&nbsp;
behavior while I was growing up."

00:02:25.901 --> 00:02:27.280
"She just thought you were playing yourself!"

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:31.920
“That's exactly right, so…&nbsp;
moms! What can I tell ya?"

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:33.920
"Oh, she used to hobble everybody!"

00:02:33.920 --> 00:02:34.420
"Yeah."

00:02:35.201 --> 00:02:38.800
Better hide your sledgehammers next&nbsp;
time Kathy Bates comes to visit!

00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:44.320
Many Zac Efron fans were disturbed to hear&nbsp;
that he would be playing serial killer Ted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:50.560
Bundy in the 2019 biopic Extremely Wicked,&nbsp;
Shockingly Evil and Vile. The role was quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.960
the departure for the Disney star-turned-comedy&nbsp;
lead. And during a screening of the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.000
Efron's mom couldn't get her head around it&nbsp;
either. As the actor recounted to Page Six,

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:04.545
"I’ve never seen my mom like that before.&nbsp;
She was so scared, she was terrified."

00:03:04.545 --> 00:03:09.360
Efron's mom's reaction actually made him&nbsp;
curious about Bundy, whom he described as:

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.560
"The most notorious, evil,&nbsp;
scumbag killer of women."

00:03:12.560 --> 00:03:16.560
Efron's mom also had opinions about&nbsp;
one of his more lighthearted roles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.600
In the 2016 comedy sequel Neighbors&nbsp;
2: Sorority Rising, his character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:26.720
coincidentally also named Teddy, distracts a&nbsp;
party full of sorority girls with a dance that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:31.840
ends with him revealing his private parts,&nbsp;
although it was actually a prosthetic prop.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:36.720
A paparazzi picture from the set made its way&nbsp;
to Efron's mom, who ended up telling her son,

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:37.966
"Have some class!"

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:44.880
There are two schools of thought regarding Robert&nbsp;
Downey Jr.'s performance in the 2008 satire Tropic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:49.600
Thunder. One says that the blackface the white&nbsp;
actor donned to play a white actor who's undergone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.600 --> 00:03:55.040
cosmetic surgery to darken his skin is a hilarious&nbsp;
commentary on the ridiculousness of actors who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:59.760
change their appearance for a role. The other&nbsp;
says that ultimately, Downey was still a white&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.680
actor in blackface playing into stereotypes about&nbsp;
Black men. Downey's mom was in the second camp.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:10.960
During a 2020 appearance on The Joe Rogan&nbsp;
Experience podcast, the actor admitted,

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:12.560
"My mother was horrified."

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:14.560
As he recalled, she told him,

00:04:14.560 --> 00:04:16.634
"I have a bad feeling about this."

00:04:16.634 --> 00:04:18.166
"I was like, 'Yeah, me too, Mom.'”

00:04:18.400 --> 00:04:23.733
Downey also explained his decision to take the&nbsp;
role despite his mother's warning, as he noted,

00:04:23.733 --> 00:04:27.040
"I get to hold up to nature the&nbsp;
insane self-involved hypocrisy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:30.560
of artists and what they think&nbsp;
they’re allowed to do on occasion."

00:04:30.560 --> 00:04:31.760
He also added,

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:35.440
"It was impossible to not have it be&nbsp;
an offensive nightmare of a movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.440 --> 00:04:39.086
and 90 percent of my Black friends&nbsp;
are like, 'Dude, that was great.'"

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:44.800
Jonah Hill's filmography doesn't exactly&nbsp;
scream "family-friendly." Movies like Superbad,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:49.280
Knocked Up, and Get Him to the Greek definitely&nbsp;
aren't exactly safe for watching with your mom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:49.280 --> 00:04:54.320
But in 2011, he proved that he could carry a&nbsp;
dramatic role that his parents would have no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:59.200
trouble watching when he picked up an Oscar&nbsp;
nomination for the baseball flick Moneyball.

00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:05.040
Then in 2013, he combined his Oscar-worthy&nbsp;
dramatic skills with his comedy background for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:10.160
crime epic The Wolf of Wall Street, in which he&nbsp;
played a financial trader who made Hill's previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:14.960
characters look like well-behaved kindergartners.&nbsp;
The actor struggled to find anything to like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:19.040
about the goldfish-eating delinquent, so&nbsp;
perhaps it's no surprise that watching it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.040 --> 00:05:24.134
with his mom wasn't exactly the best idea. As&nbsp;
he recounted on Late Night with Seth Meyers,

00:05:24.134 --> 00:05:26.800
"[My mom] sat next to me at the&nbsp;
Wolf of Wall Street premiere,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.880
and it's all fun and games until you pull&nbsp;
your d--- out of your pants. She's like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:33.932
'Alright, you know, I think I've&nbsp;
got enough of your body of work.'"

00:05:33.932 --> 00:05:35.440
"At least that part of your body."

00:05:35.440 --> 00:05:36.354
"Exactly."

00:05:36.354 --> 00:05:38.560
Hill was actually wearing&nbsp;
a prosthetic private part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.960
but that didn't exactly cover up the awkwardness.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:47.280
If you only know Aubrey Plaza as the deadpan but&nbsp;
surprisingly sincere April Ludgate on Parks and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.280 --> 00:05:52.240
Recreation, watching her movies might come as a&nbsp;
bit of a shock to you. She tends to pick projects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.240 --> 00:05:58.000
that are of a piece with April's misanthropic&nbsp;
outsider tendencies, but much more extreme.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:03.040
She played a wannabe time traveler in the&nbsp;
sci-fi comedy Safety Not Guaranteed, a zombie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.040 --> 00:06:09.360
in Life After Beth, a sadistic nun in The Little&nbsp;
Hours, and an aspiring influencer-slash-stalker&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:14.160
in Ingrid Goes West. She even got busy&nbsp;
with Robert De Niro in Dirty Grandpa!

00:06:14.160 --> 00:06:18.480
But it was the 2020 psychological&nbsp;
thriller Black Bear that truly proved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.480 --> 00:06:22.160
to be too much for Plaza's family&nbsp;
to bear. In the mind-bending film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.160 --> 00:06:27.200
she plays an actress on the edge of a breakdown&nbsp;
due to her director husband's emotional abuse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.200 --> 00:06:30.960
But it wasn't the dark plot that worried&nbsp;
her family so much as the tough production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.960 --> 00:06:34.800
conditions. When she was interviewed&nbsp;
by Adam Sandler for Interview Magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.800 --> 00:06:39.360
she revealed that the shoot was difficult and&nbsp;
involved a lot of nights. As she described it,

00:06:39.360 --> 00:06:42.240
"We were living in these weird cabins&nbsp;
in the middle of nowhere [...] The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.240 --> 00:06:46.240
lines started to get blurred toward the&nbsp;
end because we shot it chronologically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.240 --> 00:06:51.554
and I started to not really know what was&nbsp;
real or not. I really fell apart afterwards."

00:06:51.554 --> 00:06:55.680
Her family was understandably worried about&nbsp;
her because she wasn't her typical self.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:55.680 --> 00:07:00.240
Her parents did eventually see the movie,&nbsp;
although her mom was apparently horrified.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:06.332
"I'm in a very vulnerable position in the&nbsp;
movie, and it's excruc- it's painful to watch."

00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.600
In 1989, Julia Roberts was best&nbsp;
known for making moviegoers cry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:17.200
in her Oscar-nominated role as Shelby in Steel&nbsp;
Magnolias. Soon thereafter, she graduated to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.200 --> 00:07:23.840
leading lady status by taking the iconic part&nbsp;
of sex worker Vivian in 1990's Pretty Woman.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:27.840
Roberts won over audiences and&nbsp;
Richard Gere's corporate raider Edward&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.840 --> 00:07:32.560
with her bathtub karaoke, legendary&nbsp;
comeback to a snooty sales assistant,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:37.280
and combination of toughness and vulnerability.&nbsp;
But none of that changed the fact that she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.280 --> 00:07:40.880
worried about what her mom would think.&nbsp;
As she explained to Rolling Stone,

00:07:40.880 --> 00:07:46.613
"My mom works for the Catholic archdiocese of&nbsp;
Atlanta [...] I mean, my mom's boss baptized me."

00:07:46.613 --> 00:07:48.240
Hoping to avoid the inevitable,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.240 --> 00:07:52.400
Roberts tried evasive maneuvers by&nbsp;
calling her mom at work and telling her,

00:07:52.400 --> 00:07:57.490
"Hi, mom, I got a job [...] It's a Disney&nbsp;
movie! I've got to go, talk to you later.'"

00:07:57.490 --> 00:08:00.160
Pretty Woman was released by Buena Vista Pictures,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.160 --> 00:08:05.760
Disney's former film distribution studio, so she&nbsp;
technically wasn't lying. And hopefully the Best&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:05.760 --> 00:08:12.000
Lead Actress Oscar nomination she eventually got&nbsp;
for the movie helped smooth things over as well.

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:17.520
The 2012 film Flight should probably never be&nbsp;
watched by anyone who's ever felt the tiniest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:17.520 --> 00:08:22.800
sliver of fear of flying. It stars Denzel&nbsp;
Washington as pilot Whip Whitaker, who averts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:27.280
a plane crash by rolling the jet, even though he&nbsp;
was under the influence of drugs and alcohol at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:33.120
the time. Most of the passengers and crew survive,&nbsp;
but not Katerina, the flight attendant played by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.120 --> 00:08:38.080
Nadine Velazquez. It couldn't have been easy for&nbsp;
the actress' parents to watch their daughter's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:38.080 --> 00:08:43.040
character die, but that's not why she waited until&nbsp;
a month before the movie came out to tell them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.040 --> 00:08:47.840
about it. As it turns out, she was more worried&nbsp;
about what they would think of the explicit scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:52.674
of Katerina and Whip spending the night together.&nbsp;
As she explained to the Chicago Tribune,

00:08:52.674 --> 00:08:56.240
"I knew they wouldn't have understood.&nbsp;
I would have felt guilty doing it had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.240 --> 00:09:00.801
I [told them] when I got the offer.&nbsp;
So I just waited until it was done."

00:09:00.801 --> 00:09:05.112
Her parents' responses ended up being&nbsp;
what she'd expected. As she recalled,

00:09:05.112 --> 00:09:07.520
"My mom was horrified for about five seconds.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.520 --> 00:09:10.640
And my dad in the background&nbsp;
was like, 'No, you didn't.'"

00:09:10.640 --> 00:09:13.600
Since the scene in question happens&nbsp;
within the first ten minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.600 --> 00:09:15.760
she advised them to just show up late.

00:09:16.560 --> 00:09:21.360
A movie about rival ballerinas that comes out&nbsp;
during the holiday season and stars a Harvard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:26.431
graduate might sound on paper like a mom-friendly&nbsp;
choice for a family trip to the movies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.431 --> 00:09:32.320
But 2010's Black Swan is less about the dancing&nbsp;
and more about the terrifying psychological toll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:37.520
of a highly competitive athletic profession,&nbsp;
which is why Mila Kunis' mom was wary of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.520 --> 00:09:41.520
daughter's part in the film. The actress lost&nbsp;
a significant amount of weight for the part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.520 --> 00:09:45.920
to the point that her mom was legitimately&nbsp;
worried about her. As Kunis recalled to E!,

00:09:45.920 --> 00:09:48.960
"My mom freaked out. Everybody&nbsp;
started panicking. She was like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:53.280
'You have to promise me this isn't going to&nbsp;
affect you.' I was like, 'I promise it won't,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:53.280 --> 00:09:57.434
but it might take me a little time to be&nbsp;
okay with having a little more fat on me.'"

00:09:57.434 --> 00:10:02.160
The experience opened Kunis' eyes to the movie&nbsp;
industry's unhealthy concept of what makes for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.160 --> 00:10:06.400
a good-looking body. Fortunately, though,&nbsp;
she managed to keep her promise to her mom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.400 --> 00:10:11.040
as it took her a mere five days to gain&nbsp;
back the 20 pounds that she'd lost.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:15.200
If you're an actor with parents who&nbsp;
are also actors, that doesn't mean&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:18.880
they're going to automatically be&nbsp;
happy with all the roles you choose,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:24.160
as Dakota Johnson has discovered. Her parents&nbsp;
are Melanie Griffith and Don Johnson, who both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.160 --> 00:10:28.400
have their own experience with taking their&nbsp;
clothes off on screen. But when Dakota landed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.400 --> 00:10:33.760
the coveted lead role in the Fifty Shades of Grey&nbsp;
trilogy, her mom couldn't bring herself to watch.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:38.000
When mother and daughter were on the&nbsp;
red carpet together at the 2015 Oscars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:43.280
ABC's Lara Spencer asked Griffith if she would&nbsp;
see Fifty Shades of Grey, to which she replied,

00:10:43.920 --> 00:10:47.772
"I don't think I can. I&nbsp;
think it would be strange."

00:10:47.772 --> 00:10:50.480
Dakota said that it was fine that&nbsp;
her mom didn't want to watch it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.480 --> 00:10:53.954
but she still held out some hope&nbsp;
that she would change her mind.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.954 --> 00:10:57.360
When Spencer assured Griffith that her&nbsp;
daughter was very good in the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:01.840
Griffith said that she didn't need to watch&nbsp;
it to know that, to which Dakota responded,

00:11:01.840 --> 00:11:03.653
"All right! You don't have to see it!"

00:11:03.653 --> 00:11:09.200
Griffith apparently kept her word, at least for&nbsp;
a few more years. In 2018, Dakota told Hello!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:13.840
Magazine that neither of her parents had seen&nbsp;
any of the Fifty Shades movies, as she explained,

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:17.120
"It's inappropriate for them.&nbsp;
But they're okay about it."

